  abstract = {Disposal of ciprofloxacin residues into water bodies contributes to significant environmental pollution, promoting antibacterial resistance genes and serious human and animal health risks. In this study, a ZrO2-Fe3O4/GO nanocomposite was synthesized and utilized for the removal of ciprofloxacin from wastewater by the batch adsorption technique. The nanocomposites were characterized for their point of zero charge using the salt addition method, and their physicochemical properties were analyzed using state-of-the-art instruments. The batch adsorption experiments were optimized using the central composite design of response surface methodology. Under the optimized conditions - contact time, 45 min, adsorbent dose of 0.3 g/100 mL, ciprofloxacin concentration, 30 mg/L, and pH 7.0 - a maximum removal efficiency of 93.92\% and adsorption capacity of 313.48 mg/g were achieved. The isotherm and kinetic studies revealed that the adsorption was better aligned with the Freundlich and pseudo-second-order models, respectively. The Thermodynamic analysis ({$\Delta$}G, {$\Delta$}H, and {$\Delta$}S) showed that the adsorption process was spontaneous, exothermic, and favorable. Real sample adsorption study confirmed that ZrO2-Fe3O4/GO achieved a ciprofloxacin removal efficiency of 90.75\% under the optimized conditions. Moreover, the adsorbent demonstrated strong reusability, maintaining 85.45\% efficiency after eight cycles. These findings show that ZrO2-Fe3O4/GO is an effective, reusable, and magnetically separable adsorbent for ciprofloxacin removal from wastewater.  KEY WORDS: Adsorption, ZrO2-Fe3O4/GO nanocomposite, Ciprofloxacin, Wastewater, Central composite Design, Response surface methodology  Bull. Chem. Soc. Ethiop. 2026, 40(5), 1147-1171~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~  DOI: https://dx.doi.org/10.4314/bcse.v40i5.15}
}

